# OpenRobOps configs

This folder contains YAML files that need to be applied to OpenRobOps to configure data sources,
actions and other settings in OpenRobOps specific to this flatland simulation.

There are two profiles, one per agent. Apply EITHER one, never both:

| Folder  | Agent                                | Contents |
|---------|--------------------------------------|----------|
| `ros2/` | InOrbit ROS2 agent (compose `agent`) | key-value and ROS-diagnostics data sources, robot/fleet/welcome/navigation dashboards, battery/dock/message actions, ROS agentlet ModuleStates, statuses and incidents |
| `iso/`  | ISO 21423 agent (compose `iso-agent`) | data sources over the built-in attributes the ISO ingest fills (`batteryPercentage`, `batteryVoltage`, `batteryIsCharging`, `speedLinear`, `speedAngular`), the same dashboards reduced to that data, the battery status/incident, and `iso-robot.yaml` (robot admission) |

`maps.yaml` (profile-independent) uploads a rendered "pretty" version of the simulation map as a
shared `SpatialAnnotation`; the header comment records the `png2map.py` command that generated it.
Apply it with either profile — for the ISO profile it is the only map the robot has.

`footprint.yaml` (profile-independent) sets the fleet-wide `RobotFootprint` (nav2's 0.44 x 0.28 m
rectangle enlarged to 0.60 x 0.38 m, rounded corners) so the Navigation widget draws the robot's real outline instead of the default
ring. ISO robots also report theirs through the identity's `imrFootprint`; config overrides it.

Dashboards, data sources, statuses and incidents use the same ids in both profiles, so switching
profiles overwrites them in place. Objects that exist only in `ros2/` (actions, ModuleStates,
`message`/`navigationActivity`/`nav2Health`) are left behind when switching to `iso/`; clear them
with `inorbit delete -f ros2/config.yaml` first if you want a clean slate.

## Welcome page content

The Welcome dashboard uses seven text widgets across three sections:

**Intro section** (no title)

| File | Layout | Content |
|------|--------|---------|
| `welcome-intro.md` | full width | Welcome heading and OpenRobOps intro |

**Dashboards section**

| File | Layout | Content |
|------|--------|---------|
| `welcome-fleet.md` | 4 columns | Fleet dashboard description |
| `welcome-robot.md` | 4 columns | Robot dashboard description |
| `welcome-navigation.md` | 4 columns | Navigation dashboard description |

**Simulation section**

| File | Layout | Content |
|------|--------|---------|
| `welcome-simulation-map.md` | 6 columns | Simulation details and ASCII map |
| `welcome-action.md` | 6 columns | Commands and key-value telemetry |
| `welcome-footer.md` | full width | GitHub / docker environment link |

The Action widget differs per profile: `welcome-action.md` (ros2) vs `welcome-action-iso.md` (iso).
After editing any file, regenerate the profile's `config.yaml`:

```
node embed-welcome-content.mjs ros2
node embed-welcome-content.mjs iso
```

## Apply configs

To apply configs, use InOrbit's CLI. First set up `INORBIT_CLI_API_KEY` and
`INORBIT_CLI_URL` (e.g. http://oro.example.com/api) environment variables, then:
```
inorbit apply -f ros2/config.yaml                                    # InOrbit ROS2 agent
inorbit apply -f iso/config.yaml && inorbit apply -f iso/iso-robot.yaml   # ISO 21423 agent
inorbit apply -f maps.yaml                                           # shared map, either profile
inorbit apply -f footprint.yaml                                      # fleet footprint, either profile
```
